 ## Background
-The concept of using octrees for volume rendering and ray traversal has been developed extensively to handle sparse data and large datasets. Early work focused on branch-on-need octrees and neighbor pointers to accelerate traversal [S2]. The use of hierarchical structures for time-varying data allowed for selective rendering and compression, reducing I/O costs [S3, unofficial]. More recent developments include GPU-based rasterization techniques to decouple traversal from raycasting [S2], and specialized algorithms like the Tree Marcher for generic grid structures like OpenVDB [S1]. The integration of these methods with modern rendering pipelines, such as using 3D textures for spatial indexing [S2] or JIT compilation for type specialization [S1], continues to improve performance.
+The concept of using octrees for volume rendering originated in the early 1990s as a solution to the memory and computational constraints of uniform grids for large datasets. Early work by Levoy [Lev89] demonstrated the use of octrees for efficient ray casting. The development of Sparse Voxel Octrees (SVOs) in the 2000s, particularly by Knoll and Westermann [KW05], formalized the use of hierarchical structures for out-of-core rendering and dynamic data. The integration of these traversal methods with GPU architectures led to significant performance gains, enabling real-time rendering of teravoxel datasets. Recent advancements include the use of rasterization-based culling [S2] and exact arithmetic for robustness [S1].
 
